{"h": {"measure": "A", "k": 100}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 135, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Adding the input-to-output residual where none exists restores gradient flow and yields much larger gains than replacing an existing conv/pool on that edge."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "With edge 1->3 absent, node 3 has fewer inputs, so a direct skip into the output matters more."}, {"edit_edge": 3, "edit_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Filling the empty 0->3 edge creates a short path to the output, while replacing an existing op barely changes the cell."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Adding a new conv path into the output node helps much more than swapping an op that already feeds it."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When edge 2->3 is missing, edge 1->3 is the only conv route to the output, so adding a conv there gives large gains."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 3, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Without the 0->3 skip, a skip on 2->3 supplies the missing residual path; otherwise it just replaces a useful conv."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "Adding a conv on a missing edge into the output adds both capacity and connectivity, whereas replacing an existing op only changes the op type."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Removing a parameterized 3x3 conv on 0->1 loses more capacity than removing a parameter-free skip or pool."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": 